content::
The IS A relationship represents inheritance in Java, where one class inherits the properties and behaviors of another class. 
It indicates that a subclass is a specialized form of its superclass.
The IS A relationship is achieved using the extends keyword.
It promotes code reusability by allowing a child class to inherit fields and methods from a parent class.
command::
./tts.sh input.txt "90. What is the IS A relationship in OOPs Java.mp3"

content::
What are the different types of inheritance in Java?
Inheritance is of four types.
Single Inheritance. 
one subclass extends one superclass. 
Multilevel Inheritance.
subclass A extends B, and subclass B extends C, it creates a chain of inheritance called multilevel inheritance.
Hierarchical Inheritance.
multiple subclasses extend the same parent class.
Multiple Inheritance.
One subclass inherits from multiple parents.
Java does not support multiple inheritance with classes because it can cause ambiguity and complexity.
Java supports multiple inheritance through interfaces. A class can implement multiple interfaces.
command::
./tts.sh input.txt "91.What are the different types of inheritance in Java.mp3"

content::
inheritance has some limitations.
Tight coupling. Changes in the parent class can affect its child classes.
More complexity. Deep inheritance hierarchies can be difficult to understand and maintain.
No multiple inheritance with classes. A class cannot extend multiple classes in Java.
Less flexibility. Too much inheritance can make code harder to change. Sometimes composition is a better choice.
command::
./tts.sh input.txt "92.inheritance has some limitation.mp3"

content::
Inheritance is useful, but composition is often more flexible.
Composition. Part A relationship.
Why composition can be better.
Less coupling. Inheritance tightly connects the child to the parent.
More flexibility. Composition allows us to combine different objects without creating a large class hierarchy.
Easier to change. With composition, we can replace a component at runtime.
Inheritance is fixed. The parent-child relationship is decided at compile time.
constructors cannot be inherited in Java.
command::
./tts.sh input.txt "93.Inheritance is useful, but composition is often more flexible.mp3"

content::
Composition is a Part A relationship.
one class contains an object of another class and controls the child object's lifetime.
The contained object depends on the parent object. 
If the parent object is destroyed, its contained object is also destroyed.
Composition promotes code reuse and flexible design.
Aggregation. Represent it by using the empty diamond.
Composition. Represent it by using the filled diamond.
command::
./tts.sh input.txt "94.Composition.mp3"

content::
Polymorphism. One thing, many forms.
The same method name can have different behaviors.
Java achieves polymorphism through method overloading and method overriding.
It makes code more flexible, reusable, and easier to maintain.
Method Overloading. Compile time polymorphism.
Same method name, different parameters.
Decided by the compiler.
Method Overriding. Runtime polymorphism.
Child class provides its own implementation of a parent method.
Decided at runtime.
command::
./tts.sh input.txt "95.Polymorphism.mp3"

content::
Runtime polymorphism or Dynamic Method Dispatch is a feature where the method to execute is decided at runtime based on the actual object, not the reference type.
It allows a parent class reference to call an overridden method of a child class.
Achieved through method overriding.
A parent reference can refer to a child object.
The child's overridden method is executed at runtime.
command::
./tts.sh input.txt "96.Runtime polymorphism or Dynamic Method Dispatch.mp3"

content::
Method Overriding is when a child class provides its own implementation of a method that already exists in the parent class.
Used to achieve runtime polymorphism in Java.
command::
./tts.sh input.txt "97.Method Overriding.mp3"

content::
Method Overloading.
a class has multiple methods with the same name but different parameters.
Methods must differ in the number of parameters, type of parameters, or order of parameters.
It allows the same method name to handle different types of input.
Used to achieve compile-time polymorphism in Java.
